  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+8x-6y+21=0$",
  "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+8x-6y+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}+8x-6\\cdot 0+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+21 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+16+21 & =0+16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+21 & =16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+4)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+4)^{2}=-5.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/53b8e33665ee3ba0b36d04c9d82715b18505c501db535ecf4a40c48c81814584.png)",
